### Background Service Worker for Manifest V3

Source: https://context7.com/crxjs/chrome-extension-tools/llms.txt

A basic example of a background service worker for a Manifest V3 Chrome Extension. It includes listeners for installation events, message handling from content scripts, and context menu creation.

```typescript
declare const self: ServiceWorkerGlobalScope
export {}

// Listen for extension installation
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener((details) => {
  if (details.reason === 'install') {
    chrome.runtime.openOptionsPage()
  }
})

// Handle messages from content scripts
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (message.type === 'GET_TAB_INFO') {
    sendResponse({ tabId: sender.tab?.id, url: sender.tab?.url })
  }
  return true // Keep channel open for async response
})

// Context menu example
chrome.contextMenus.create({
  id: 'myExtension',
  title: 'Process with My Extension',
  contexts: ['selection'],
})
```

--------------------------------

### Declare Content Scripts in manifest.json

Source: https://github.com/crxjs/chrome-extension-tools/blob/main/packages/vite-plugin-docs/docs/getting-started/solid/02-add-content-script.md

This JSON snippet shows how to declare content scripts in the manifest.json file. It specifies the JavaScript files to execute and the URL patterns where the content script should be injected.

```json
{
  // other fields...
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "js": ["src/content.jsx"],
      "matches": ["https://www.google.com/*"]
    }
  ]
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configure Service Worker in manifest.json

Source: https://github.com/crxjs/chrome-extension-tools/blob/main/packages/vite-plugin-docs/docs/concepts/02-background.md

Defines the background service worker entry point and sets the module type in the manifest. This configuration is required for CRXJS to properly bundle and load the background script as an ES module.

```json
{
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "src/background.ts",
    "type": "module"
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Import Static Assets in Content Scripts

Source: https://context7.com/crxjs/chrome-extension-tools/llms.txt

Demonstrates importing static assets like images and CSS directly within content scripts. CRXJS handles the declaration of web-accessible resources automatically. `chrome.runtime.getURL()` is used to obtain the correct extension URL for these assets.

```typescript
// src/content/main.ts
import logoUrl from './assets/logo.png'
import styles from './styles.css?inline'

// Convert to extension URL for use in DOM
const extensionLogoUrl = chrome.runtime.getURL(logoUrl)

const img = document.createElement('img')
img.src = extensionLogoUrl
img.alt = 'Extension Logo'

// Inject styles
const styleElement = document.createElement('style')
styleElement.textContent = styles
document.head.appendChild(styleElement)

document.body.appendChild(img)
```

--------------------------------

### Configure Vite for CRXJS

Source: https://github.com/crxjs/chrome-extension-tools/blob/main/packages/vite-plugin-docs/docs/getting-started/react/00-create-project.md

Updates the Vite configuration file (vite.config.js) to include the CRXJS plugin and load the extension's manifest. This enables Vite to build the Chrome Extension correctly.

```javascript
import { defineConfig } from 'vite'
import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'
import { crx } from '@crxjs/vite-plugin'
import manifest from './manifest.json'

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    react(),
    crx({ manifest }),
  ],
})
```
